# C1 control run, arm64-apple-darwin

rustc 1.98.0-nightly (57d06900f 2026-05-27)
rustc --edition 2024 -O --emit asm c1_a_runtime_selection_must_leave_a_branch.rs -o c1.s

## Program output (the premise: all three agree on values, the model selects)

fold_const   = 520
fold_runtime = 520  (same weighting, arriving at runtime)
fold_direct  = 520  (the arm, hand written)
values agree: yes
fold_runtime at a weighting preferring accuracy = 21000
the weighting selects: yes

The verdict is in the assembly, not here. Count conditional jumps
in `fold_const` and in `fold_runtime`: the control passes when the
first has none and the second has them, and fails when they match,
because then the comparison cannot tell the two apart at all.

## The verdict, read off c1.s

fold_const       9 instructions,  2 of them branches
fold_runtime    76 instructions, 11 of them branches
fold_direct      9 instructions,  2 of them branches

fold_const against fold_direct, after label normalisation:
  identical

## What this establishes

The const arm lowers to the hand-written arm exactly: nine instructions,
and its two branches are the loop rather than the selection. The runtime
arm, whose only difference is where the weighting comes from, lowers to
seventy-six instructions carrying eleven branches and all three arms.

So the comparison instrument CAN come out both ways. That is the case that
had to fail, and it fails, which is what the three probes backing the
no-residue finding never supplied.
